INT. VELODROME - INFIELD - EARLY MORNING

An enormous oval of pale wooden track banked to a steep tilt, lit by a few high lamps. The empty seats rise in tiers into the dark. The air smells of rubber and resin.

ISOLDE MAKINEN (20s, skin suit, helmet hanging from one hand, a scar on her left calf) sits on an upturned crate, unlacing her cleats. Her bike leans against the rail, its carbon frame catching the lamplight like a blade.

She stares at the track. Doesn't blink. Doesn't speak. One thumb works at a loose thread on her glove.

A phone on the crate beside her buzzes. She glances at the screen, then back at the track. It buzzes again. She answers, thumb on speaker.

COACH (V.O.)
(over phone)
You there?

ISOLDE
Yes.

COACH (V.O.)
Sleep?

ISOLDE
Some.

COACH (V.O.)
Eat?

ISOLDE
A banana.

COACH (V.O.)
That's not eating.

She says nothing. Behind her eyes, something flickers: a thousand laps, a thousand pairs of hands, a wall of sound.

COACH (V.O.) (CONT'D)
You ready?

Isolde stands. Sets down her cleats. Steps toward the bike. Lays a palm on the saddle.

ISOLDE
Ask me in four minutes.

She hangs up.
